The generator places lessons into periods for you, respecting lessons-per-week, teacher
availability and the daily and consecutive limits. It does not invent anything — it can only place
what **Class Subjects** already says exists.

## Before you start

Everything below must already be true. The generator checks the first two for you and refuses to run
otherwise:

1. A [bell schedule](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-bell-schedule) exists.
2. [Class-subject assignments](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-class-subjects) exist — with teachers.
3. Teacher availability and limits are set in **Timetable → Configuration → Teacher Constraints**.
   Skip this and the generator assumes everyone is free all week.

## Steps

1. Open **Timetable → Timetable Grid**.
2. Select **Generate**. The **Generate Timetable** dialog opens with two preflight rows at the top:
   - **Bell schedule configured** (or *Bell schedules configured: N* if you have more than one)
   - **Class-subject assignments: *N***

   If either fails you get **Complete setup in Timetable Configuration before generating**, and the
   Generate button stays disabled. Fix the setup rather than trying to work around it.
3. Choose the **Scope**:
   - **All classes** — the normal choice for a fresh timetable.
   - **Selected classes only** — then tick the classes. Use this when you are re-doing one year
     group and don't want the rest touched.
4. Decide on **Replace existing slots**:
   - **Off** (default) — the generator works around what is already placed.
   - **On** — existing slots in scope are **deleted** first. The dialog says so explicitly. This is
     the right choice for a genuine rebuild and the wrong one if a colleague has hand-placed
     anything.
5. **Strongly recommended the first time:** turn on **Preview only (don't save)**. The button changes
   from **Generate** to **Preview**, and you get the full result without anything being written.
6. Run it.

## Reading the result

The result sheet shows:

- a **score** as a percentage — how well the placement satisfied the constraints,
- **Scheduled** — lessons placed,
- **Skipped** — lessons it could not place,
- **Total**,
- a list of **warnings** explaining the skips.

Then three choices:

- **Accept & Save** — commit the result.
- **Discard** — throw it away and change something.
- **View Timetable** — look at the grid before deciding.

A preview run only offers you the look; nothing is saved either way.

## When lessons get skipped

Skips are almost always a constraint that cannot be satisfied, not a failure of the generator. In
rough order of likelihood:

- **Not enough periods in the day** for the lessons-per-week the curriculum demands. Either add
  periods to the bell schedule or reduce lessons/week.
- **A teacher is over-committed** — the same person is the only one assigned to a subject in too
  many classes.
- **Teacher constraints are too tight** — availability windows, max/day or max consecutive leave no
  legal slot.
- **A subject has no teacher** in Class Subjects, so there is no one to place.

Read the warnings, fix the cause, and re-run in preview until the skipped count is acceptable.

## Finishing by hand

The generator gets you most of the way; the last few slots are usually manual. In the grid:

- Select a cell to set the lesson there, or place a whole **subject group** where classes split into
  options at the same time.
- **Copy day** repeats one day onto others, and tells you how many slots copied and how many were
  **skipped as conflicts** — nothing is silently overwritten.
- The grid refuses any change that double-books a teacher or a room, and highlights the clash.
- Filter the view by **Room**, **Teacher** or **Class**, and by day, using the toolbar.

